**Proposition 8.2 (the remaining constructive boundary is specific).**
An implementation preserving immediate original-action disabledness
must distinguish at admission the canonical RD/IN cuts of Proposition
2.3. A completion-call interface allowing paid preliminary checks
must instead expose its checking/refusal cuts and their different
action interpretation. Either version of the complete-row bridge
must, on the same initialized source, supply current executable
continuation that completes the actual callback TOK of
Proposition 3.4 and final halt of Proposition 3.5, and carries the
accepted input of Proposition 2.1 through its unique original receipt
and matching WR as in Proposition 4.4. If it permits a completed
bad raw activation, it must additionally complete DONE at every
$\mathsf{Load}_v\land\mathsf{Blocked}_v$ instance. A predecessor
decoder or an output-fusion alone cannot satisfy these requirements.

Proof. The admission alternatives follow from Proposition 2.3's
strict disabledness contradiction and its stated escape conditions;
the next two requirements are the direct self-invalidating
guards; the input requirement follows from Propositions 2.1–2.2 and
the exact balance theorem. The code clause is Theorem 3.3 with its
actual loader and disabledness premises retained. Output fusion
changes none of these admission/input/permission predicates.
Predecessor reconstruction changes no actual executable guard,
wire word or completed receipt. Thus neither operation supplies
the missing cases. ∎

